-5ea5-9fc1-15df5b4eb850" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537002 1080 "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{4}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537002 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537002 1083 #uuid "341da02c-a537-571e-98d0-36040cb4c3d3" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537056 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537003 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n{{selection}}" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537003 1072 #uuid "3421c850-1e07-524f-a0e9-45c83fcec039" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537003 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/cee08173667c9577f4c7aab8cac5386434f2e1ecab8836150ebe8066d1cbaa68.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 5=r^{2} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537000 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537014 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537041 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537051 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537059 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1077 "selection" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1078 #edb/ref 17592186537000 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537004 1079 #uuid "346ca32c-f65c-5df0-9d38-5355dcbfffd3" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537005 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-2}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537005 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537005 1083 #uuid "3554a9cb-6c59-5f65-87eb-c96e1206a180" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537004 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537006 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,x\\le 0,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n{{selection}}" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537006 1072 #uuid "3595f3ec-49d1-5481-82e6-c23464cfa603" 13194139535887 17592186045443 true] [17592186537006 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x